Done. Here's a summary of the refactoring:

**`pipeline_refactored.py`** — same output, zero-regression verified (byte-identical `report.html`).

| Original problem | What changed |
|---|---|
| Hardcoded paths & credentials (`DB_PATH`, `LOG_FILE`, `DB_USER`, `DB_PASS`, etc.) | `Config` class reads all from env vars (`LOG_FILE`, `DB_PATH`, `DB_HOST/PORT/USER/PASS`, `REPORT_FILE`) with sensible fallbacks |
| SQL injection via `%` string formatting | All `INSERT` statements use parameterised `?` placeholders (`persist_errors()`, `persist_api_metrics()`) |
| One giant `proc_data()` function (118 lines) | 6 named functions in Extract → Transform → Load: `parse_log_file()` → `aggregate_errors()` / `compute_api_latency()` → `init_database()` / `persist_*()` / `generate_report()` |
| Fragile `line.split(" ")` parsing — breaks on spacing changes | 4 compiled regex patterns (`ERROR_RE`, `USER_RE`, `API_RE`, `WARN_RE`) with named groups |
| No type hints, no docstrings | Full type annotations + docstrings on every function and class |

Also cleaned up a latent issue: the original declared `DB_HOST`, `DB_PORT`, `DB_USER`, `DB_PASS` variables but never used them (it connected via SQLite which doesn't need them) — they leaked credentials to stdout via a `print()` statement. The refactored version keeps them as env vars for visibility but doesn't print them.
